# Antipodes and Axes: An Interactive Journey

Begin with the central question: can an antipode-preserving simplicial map compress one cross-polytope sphere into another? Adjust the dimensions below and watch axis counting answer.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## The complete existence landscape

The criterion is not probabilistic: the map exists exactly in the region m ≤ n. Explore the finite matrix, then compare it with the rendered heatmap.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

The constructive side is supplied by the standard equatorial inclusion.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

## Why candidate maps fail

Equivariance leaves one signed target choice per positive source axis. Simpliciality then asks only whether target coordinates are distinct. Try creating and repairing collisions.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## Coindex and suspension

The largest source dimension mapping into C_n is n itself. Suspension adds one source axis and one target axis, preserving existence while increasing coindex by one.

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

## Counting the maps

Coordinate injections and independent sign choices predict rapid factorial-exponential growth below the threshold and zero maps above it.

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

This progression connects the local collision rule to the global Borsuk–Ulam obstruction and the sharp suspension law.

